{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChHDotYtu1akdoEikngzp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This isn't a bug, so I haven't written a repro script.\n\nDaylight saving time ended in Toronto at 02:00 on Sunday 1 November 2026, and the clocks went back to 01:00. That means the hour from 01:00 to 02:00 happened twice that night.\n\n- 23:00 on 31 October was EDT (UTC−4), which is 03:00 UTC.\n- 07:00 on 1 November was EST (UTC−5), which is 12:00 UTC.\n- From 03:00 to 12:00 UTC is **9 hours** of real time.\n\nThe clock times say 8 hours, but you were actually in bed for 9. `Night.duration` in `src/nightjar/models.py:52` converts both times to UTC before subtracting them, which is the right way to handle this. I ran the same calculation with `zoneinfo` and it also gave 9:00:00.\n\nIf the app had shown 8 hours, that would have been the bug: subtracting the clock times directly gives the wrong answer on DST nights."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":262,"cache_read_input_tokens":23371,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":262},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd461958-41a1-4493-94a0-62a9fb8aeeed","uuid":"fa018fda-e80c-4494-baeb-8422d1c8f37d","timestamp":"2026-11-22T03:01:52.397Z","request_id":"req_011ChHDotKz9LPwa3KPfhWpE"}
